  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1010",
      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Kryptopterus cryptopterus (Bleeker, 1851)</li><li>Common name: Blue Sheatfish</li><li>Type locality: Banjarmasin, South Kalimantan, Borneo, Indonesia</li><li>Size: 200 mm (7.9 in) SL</li><li>IUCN Red List: Least Concern (last assessed 2019)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1010",
      "html": "<p>Asia: Mekong and Chao Phraya basins; also from the Malay Peninsula, Sumatra and Borneo.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1010",
      "html": "<ul><li>pH: 6.0 - 7.0</li><li>Temperature: 22.0-27.0°C (71.6-80.6°F)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1010",
      "html": "<p>This fish is fairly active and needs open spaces to swim in. Provide plenty of vegetation for it to hide in during the day.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1010",
      "html": "<p>Readily feeds on live/frozen foods and other prepared foods such as pellets.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1010",
      "html": "<p>A schooling species that should be kept in groups of three or more. A relatively peaceful fish compatible with most other species. Ideal tankmates include larger barbs and rasboras, and other mid-sized bottom-dwelling Asian catfishes (e.g. Mystus).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1010",
      "html": "<p>Males are more slender and have strong serrations on the posterior edge of the pectoral spine (females lack these serrations).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1010",
      "html": "<p>In the Mekong, this species spawns during the early part of the rainy season (June-July). The young move toward the seasonally flooded habitats and are first seen in August. Not reported in aquaria.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1010",
      "html": "<p>Identification: rudimentary dorsal fin; the maxillary barbels reaching to the base of the pectoral fin; 64-78 anal rays. Nearly straight dorsal profile with no nuchal concavity; pectoral-fin length greater than head length.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1010",
      "html": "<p>Natuurkundig Tijdschrift voor Nederlandsch Indië v. 1 (no. 3), pp 270.</p>"
    }
  ],
